#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "e51c2aa9-027b-5b23-bcaf-805804a8de9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6099" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}y \\, \\text{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{2}  \\int_{0}^{1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y\\,  \\text{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}x \\, \\text{d}y$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x,y)=e^{\\sqrt{xy}}$ is continuous on $D=[0,2]\\times[0,1]$. In II and III, each integration interval belongs to the variable being integrated; Fubini's theorem makes both expressions equal to $\\iint_D f\\,dA$.\n\nIn I, the integral is over the reflected rectangle $D^*=[0,1]\\times[0,2]$. Set $u=y$ and $v=x$. The absolute Jacobian is $1$ and $f(v,u)=f(u,v)$, so\n\n$$\\int_0^1\\int_0^2 e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dy\\,dx\n=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2 e^{\\sqrt{uv}}\\,du\\,dv\n=\\iint_D e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA.$$\n\nAll three displayed equalities are therefore true. The source's \"II and III only\" answer correctly recognizes the two integrals with bounds directly assigned to the stated coordinates, but overlooks the equality of I caused by symmetry."}]}
